From 2dc67887f4efd1019eb281c13b0c9d1176332aee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:20:39 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add opts.getAuthorization, a callback that supplies the
 Authorization header

A caller can pass an async function that is given { regKey, uri } (an object, so that fields can be added later) and returns the value of the Authorization header, or nothing. It is asked for the keys a stored credential for the request would be looked up under, longest first: the keys of the request's URL, then, for a URL on the registry's own host (a tarball), the keys of the registry. The first value returned is sent. The walk ends at the first key that holds a stored credential which sets Authorization (a token, _auth, or a username and password), so the function wins over a stored credential for the same key and a credential stored under a longer key wins over the function's for a shorter one. A key that holds only a client certificate and key sets no Authorization, so an mTLS pair at a longer key does not shadow the function at a shorter one.

The value is sent as a stored credential is: same transport options, same http: behaviour and same redirects, and the otp retry asks again. It is not asked for an offline request or when forceAuth is given. An error thrown by the function rejects the request unchanged. A body that is a promise or a stream and fails while the function runs rejects the request as well, and a stream is piped into gzip only once the function is done. An aborted opts.signal rejects with AbortError before or during the wait, stops further key lookups, and removes its listeners once settled.

getAuth.supportsGetAuthorization is true, so a caller that cannot do without the option can tell a library that ignores it.

The library runs no program: a caller that wants a credential from one, such as npm with a configured tokenHelper, does that in the function.
